# ============================================================================
#  snug.options — example template
# ============================================================================
#
#  This is the example `snug.options` file that `snug --init-options`
#  writes to disk. Drop it in your project root and edit any flag below:
#
#      snug --init-options            # writes ./snug.options (refuses to
#                                    #   overwrite without --force)
#      snug --init-options --force    # overwrite an existing file
#      snug --init-options build.cfg  # write to a custom path
#      snug --init-options --stdout   # print to stdout (for piping or
#                                    #   version control)
#
#  Format
#  ------
#  - One option per line, parsed as if it were supplied on the command
#    line. So `--name "My App"` works — quoting and shell-style escapes
#    are honoured (the file is read with the `shell-words` tokenizer).
#  - Lines starting with `#` are comments.
#  - Command-line flags always override file values.
#  - Repeatable flags (`--jvm-arg`, `--localization`) accumulate from
#    both sources.
#
#  Most lines below are commented out — uncomment the ones you need and
#  replace the placeholder values. Anything left commented is ignored.
# ============================================================================


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Input / output
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Either a single fat-JAR file or a directory containing JAR files.
# `--input` and the positional `[JAR]` argument are mutually exclusive;
# supply one or the other. The positional form is also accepted:
#
#     MyApp.jar
#
# --input /path/to/jar-or-directory-of-jars


# --output MyApp.exe
# --name "My App"
# --company "SynapticLoop"
# --version 1.2.3
# --description "A short description of My App"
# --copyright "© 2026 SynapticLoop"


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Java entry point
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Default: read from the JAR manifest's `Main-Class` header. Override
# when the manifest is missing, when the fat JAR is multi-JAR and you
# want to pin a specific class, or when the JAR ships a different
# `Main-Class` than the one you want users to see.
#
# --main-class com.example.Main


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum Java version + JVM arguments
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Defaults to 25. Raise or lower to match your actual runtime target.
#
# --min-java 25

# Repeatable. Anything you'd put after `java -jar`. Common picks:
#
# --jvm-arg -Xms256m
# --jvm-arg -Xmx2g
# --jvm-arg -Dfile.encoding=UTF-8
# --jvm-arg -Djava.library.path=./native


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows metadata (version resource, icon, manifest)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Accepts `.png` or `.ico`. PNGs are converted to ICO at build time via
# the `image` crate. Square images ≥ 256x256 look sharpest at every DPI.
#
# --icon assets/icon.png

# Optional application manifest (XML). Used to declare DPI-awareness,
# side-by-side assembly identity, or `requestedExecutionLevel` for UAC.
#
# --manifest manifest.xml


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splash
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PNG shown by the native launcher before the JVM starts. Recommended
# size 480x270..640x360. Anything larger triggers a build-time warning.
#
# --splash assets/splash.png
# --splash-ms 1500
# --splash-max 640x360


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JDK download flow
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Pop a TaskDialog on first run if no compatible JVM is on the system,
# offering to download Eclipse Temurin from api.adoptium.net. The
# launcher verifies SHA-256, extracts to
# `%LOCALAPPDATA%\snug\jdk\<version>\`, and retries.
#
# Modes:
#   omitted — no download flow at all (developer build).
#   --download-jdk       — `auto`: prompt only if discovery fails.
#   --download-jdk=auto  — same as above, explicit.
#   --download-jdk=force — always prompt, bypassing JVM discovery
#                          (portable end-user build).
#
# --download-jdk=auto


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Localization
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Repeatable. Each `snug-localisations.<tag>.txt` file is embedded in
# the launcher; the user's Windows UI language picks the matching
# bundle. The built-in English baseline is always embedded, so a build
# with no `--localization` still ships a fully localized (English)
# launcher.
#
# Filename convention: `snug-localisations.<tag>.txt`, where `<tag>`
# is a BCP 47 locale (`en`, `en-US`, `pt-BR`, `de`, `ja`, ...).
# BCP 47 with regional variants is supported: `snug-localisations.en.txt`
# covers `en-US`, `en-GB`, etc.; `snug-localisations.en-US.txt` adds
# region-specific overrides on top.
#
# --localization i18n/snug-localisations.de.txt
# --localization i18n/snug-localisations.fr.txt


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Advanced
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# --emit-payload
#   Write the encoded payload bytes to stdout instead of building an
#   EXE. Useful for piping into other tools or for diffing payloads.
#
# --dry-run
#   Validate inputs and print what would be built, but write nothing.
#
# --options /path/to/another/options
#   Read a different options file instead of `./snug.options`.
#
# --snug-version
#   Print snug's own version (distinct from `--version`, which sets
#   the wrapped app's version).